Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell** audit of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, calibrated for integration tests.

---

## Summary

**8 tests scanned · 3 smells detected (1 Critical, 1 High, 1 Medium) · 16 of 19 catalog smells clean**

Overall this is a solid integration-test class. Most tests follow clear AAA structure, use descriptive names, properly isolate via in-memory SQLite, and clean up resources. Three findings need attention.

---

## Critical

### 1. Empty Test (testsmells.org: *Empty Test*)
**Location:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` — line 113  
**Problem:** Exercises 100 inserts but asserts nothing. This test passes even if every insert silently fails, giving false confidence.

```csharp
// ❌ Before — assertion-free
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}

// ✅ After — verify the inserts actually persisted
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

---

## High

### 2. Sleepy Test (testsmells.org: *Sleepy Test*)
**Location:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` — line 87  
**Problem:** `Thread.Sleep(3000)` is a hard wall-clock wait. It makes the test slow (3 s minimum) and flaky — if the system is slower than 3 s the test fails; if it's faster, you wasted time. Additionally, the method is `async Task` but blocks a thread with `Thread.Sleep` instead of `await Task.Delay`.  
**Integration calibration:** Real delays are expected in integration tests, but a polling loop with a timeout is the correct pattern — not a fixed sleep.

```csharp
// ❌ Before — fixed 3-second sleep
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));

// ✅ After — poll with timeout
var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(200);
var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;

while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
{
    if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
        break;
    await Task.Delay(interval);
}

Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
    "Notification was not sent within the timeout period.");
```

### 3. Conditional Test Logic (testsmells.org: *Conditional Test Logic*)
**Location:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` — lines 101-108  
**Problem:** `if/else` inside a test means different executions assert different things. If the type is `PremiumUser`, the name is never checked; if it isn't, the discount rate is never checked. A failing branch can hide behind the passing one.

```csharp
// ❌ Before — conditional assertion
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);

// ✅ After — one test per scenario
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    // Arrange a PremiumUser via the appropriate factory/insert
    // ...
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

## Medium

### 4. Assertion Roulette (testsmells.org: *Assertion Roulette*)
**Location:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` — lines 35-38 (4 assertions, no messages)  
**Problem:** If the second `AreEqual` fails, the MSTest runner reports the line but not *which* property was wrong. Adding a message to each assertion improves failure diagnostics.

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should exist after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name mismatch");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email mismatch");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be auto-assigned");
```

---

## Smells Not Detected (15 of 19 — Clean ✅)

| # | testsmells.org Smell | Status | Note |
|---|----------------------|--------|------|
| 1 | Constructor Initialization | ✅ Clean | Uses `[TestInitialize]` / `[TestCleanup]` correctly |
| 2 | Default Test | ✅ Clean | No auto-generated placeholder tests |
| 3 | Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean | No redundant repeated assertions |
| 4 | Eager Test | ✅ Clean | Each test verifies one behavior |
| 5 | Exception Handling | ✅ Clean | No `try`/`catch` in tests |
| 6 | General Fixture | ✅ Clean | Both `_connection` and `_repository` used by every test |
| 7 | Ignored Test | ✅ Clean | No `[Ignore]` annotations |
| 8 | Lazy Test | ✅ Clean | Tests are distinct, not re-testing the same method identically |
| 9 | Magic Number Test | ✅ Clean | Literals like `3` and `10` are self-evident from context |
| 10 | Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean | In-memory SQLite is an accepted integration-test fixture — no external file/network dependencies |
| 11 | Print Statement | ✅ Clean | No `Console.WriteLine` / `Debug.WriteLine` |
